      "message": "We\u0027ve gained a little bit more belief in this being roughly correct.\n\nTested on a 5.10 running phone:\n\n$ adb shell iptables-save | egrep IDLETIMER\n-A idletimer_raw_PREROUTING -i rmnet0 -j IDLETIMER --timeout 10 --label 0 --send_nl_msg\n-A idletimer_mangle_POSTROUTING -o rmnet0 -j IDLETIMER --timeout 10 --label 0 --send_nl_msg\n\n$ adb shell ip6tables-save | egrep IDLETIMER\n-A idletimer_raw_PREROUTING -i rmnet0 -j IDLETIMER --timeout 10 --label 0 --send_nl_msg\n-A idletimer_mangle_POSTROUTING -o rmnet0 -j IDLETIMER --timeout 10 --label 0 --send_nl_msg\n\n(if wifi is enabled the above switches to wlan0 and label 1, timeout 15, it doesn\u0027t appear to ever use both at once - that seems like a framework bug)\n\n$ adb shell iptables-save | egrep IDLETIMER\n-A idletimer_raw_PREROUTING -i wlan0 -j IDLETIMER --timeout 15 --label 1 --send_nl_msg\n-A idletimer_mangle_POSTROUTING -o wlan0 -j IDLETIMER --timeout 15 --label 1 --send_nl_msg\n\n$ adb shell ip6tables-save | egrep IDLETIMER\n-A idletimer_raw_PREROUTING -i wlan0 -j IDLETIMER --timeout 15 --label 1 --send_nl_msg\n-A idletimer_mangle_POSTROUTING -o wlan0 -j IDLETIMER --timeout 15 --label 1 --send_nl_msg\n\nThis then results in kernel uevents being delivered (not netlink messages as the flag suggests - we should probably rename things to just --send_event).\n\nThis makes it into system/netd/server/NetlinkHandler.cpp:\n\nvoid NetlinkHandler::onEvent(NetlinkEvent *evt) {\n...\nif (!strcmp(subsys, \"xt_idletimer\")) {\n        const char *label \u003d evt-\u003efindParam(\"INTERFACE\");\n        const char *state \u003d evt-\u003efindParam(\"STATE\");\n        const char *timestamp \u003d evt-\u003efindParam(\"TIME_NS\");\n        const char *uid \u003d evt-\u003efindParam(\"UID\");\n        if (state) {\n            bool isActive \u003d !strcmp(\"active\", state);\n            int64_t processTimestamp \u003d (timestamp \u003d\u003d nullptr) ? 0 : strtoll(timestamp, nullptr, 10);\n            int intLabel;\n            // NMS only accepts interface class activity changes with integer labels, and only ever\n            // creates idletimers with integer labels.\n            if (android::base::ParseInt(label, \u0026intLabel)) {\n                const long reportedUid \u003d\n                        (uid !\u003d nullptr \u0026\u0026 isActive) ? strtol(uid, nullptr, 10) : -1;\n                notifyInterfaceClassActivityChanged(intLabel, isActive, processTimestamp,\n                                                    reportedUid);\n            }\n        }\n\nYes the function appears to talk about netlink, but apparently netlink events get converted into uevent like things first and merged with real uevents - or something like that...\n\n$ adb shell dumpsys netd | egrep onInterfaceClassActivityChanged\n\nshows:\n\n      06-11 11:17:31.631 onInterfaceClassActivityChanged(true, 0, 28424164152306, 0) (0.05ms)\n      06-11 11:17:42.631 onInterfaceClassActivityChanged(false, 0, 28435164177905, -1) (0.09ms)\n      06-11 11:17:42.699 onInterfaceClassActivityChanged(true, 0, 28435232231331, 0) (0.1ms)\n      06-11 11:17:54.387 onInterfaceClassActivityChanged(true, 0, 28446920158949, 0) (0.1ms)\n      06-11 11:18:05.164 onInterfaceClassActivityChanged(false, 0, 28457696094542, -1) (0.2ms)\n\n$ adb shell \u0027cat /proc/uptime; date\u0027\n28848.85 189436.59\nFri Jun 11 11:24:36 PDT 2021\n\n(this date is 6 minutes 31 seconds after the log line above)\n$ echo $[28848 - 6 * 60 - 31]\n28457\n(which roughly matches the above ns timestamps)\n\nThe messages are usually repeated a number of times each (~5).\nLorenzo says this is because the event is logged per listening app, instead of directly when it happens.\n\nThe first argument is active\u003dtrue/false.  (sometimes active true happens twice in a row...)\n\nThe second is the \u0027label\u0027 and it must be an integer (ie. the iptables configured label string is converted into an integer).\n\nThe third argument is a timestamp of the event measured in nanoseconds since boot - presumably using the https://linux.die.net/man/2/clock_gettime CLOCK_BOOTTIME time scale.\n\nThe fourth argument is the uid, and will be -1 if active\u003dfalse.\n\nOn an idle phone things should log active\u003dfalse after around 10-15 seconds (based on experience).\n\n$ adbo shell ps | egrep com.google.android.gms\n...\nu0_a174      27910   720 15408600 216444 do_epoll_wait      0 S com.google.android.gms\n\ngms has internet access, u0_a174 is the id it\u0027s running as.\n\n$ adb shell su u0_a174 ping -n -c 1 -w 2 1.1.1.1\nor\n$ adb shell su u0_a174 ping6 -n -c 1 -w 2 2000::\n\nand that should cause a wakeup event from uid 10174 to show up in the above log.\n\nIdleness seems much easier to achieve on wifi with a disconnected phone, but in general this part is finicky.\n\n$ adb shell svc wifi enable\n\nRetry with ping, results in:\n\n      06-11 11:38:18.859 onInterfaceClassActivityChanged(false, 1, 29671392060101, -1) (0.1ms)\n      06-11 11:38:30.264 onInterfaceClassActivityChanged(true, 1, 29682797396288, 10174) (0.1ms)\n\nSo label\u003d1 is wifi, it first went idle, then the ping running as uid u0_a174 (ie. 10174) woke things up.\n",
